<h2>The award and the bypass attempt</h2>
<p>R.K. Automotives, a supplier of lubricants, took its delayed-payment claim to the Micro and Small Enterprise Facilitation Council. The buyer neither appeared nor replied, and on 30 October 2021 the Council passed an ex parte order for Rs. 24,04,061 — principal plus statutory interest. Rather than challenge the award through the route the statute prescribes, the buyer invoked Article 226, pleading improper service and breach of Section 18&rsquo;s two-stage procedure of conciliation followed by arbitration.</p>

<h2>What the Court held</h2>
<p>Three findings dispose of the petition. On service, the Council&rsquo;s own order recorded transmission of notice through the MSME Samadhaan portal and by messaging, with the notice viewed; non-appearance thereafter was a choice, not a denial of hearing. On procedure, compliance with Sections 18(2) and 18(3) turns on the Council&rsquo;s record, which disclosed no jurisdictional defect. On forum, the Court invoked the settled architecture: <em>&ldquo;the writ jurisdiction of this Court is not intended to substitute for the statutory forum&rdquo;</em> — a challenge under Section 34 of the 1996 Act read with Section 19 of the MSMED Act, with its 75% pre-deposit — while candidly acknowledging that <em>&ldquo;the rule of exhaustion of alternative remedies is a rule of judicial discretion and not an absolute bar.&rdquo;</em> An earlier writ petition filed and withdrawn on the same subject weighed further against indulgence. The merits of the supplier&rsquo;s claim were left untouched.</p>

<h2>The buyer&rsquo;s real options before the Council</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Respond on Samadhaan</strong> — the portal is the Council&rsquo;s service channel; monitoring it is part of contract hygiene for any buyer dealing with registered MSMEs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Contest in conciliation</strong> — quality disputes, debit notes and set-offs are best raised when the Section 18(2) conciliation begins, on the record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Defend the arbitration</strong> — if conciliation fails, the Section 18(3) arbitration is the forum for evidence; absence invites an ex parte award.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Challenge with the pre-deposit</strong> — Section 19 makes a 75% deposit the price of a Section 34 challenge; writ petitions seeking to dodge it fail in all but genuinely jurisdictional cases.</div>
</div>

<h2>The narrow writ window that survives</h2>
<p>Judgments across High Courts keep a small aperture open for Article 226: an MSEFC acting wholly without jurisdiction — for instance, against an entity never a &ldquo;buyer,&rdquo; or on a claim outside the Act — or in the teeth of natural justice demonstrated from the record. This case illustrates how narrow the aperture is in practice: a service record on the statutory portal defeats the natural-justice plea, and procedural grievances about the conciliation-arbitration sequence are tested on the Council&rsquo;s file, not on affidavit assertions.</p>
<div class="note">For suppliers, the decision underlines the strength of a Samadhaan paper trail; for buyers, the cost of ignoring it. The MSMED Act, 2006 overrides inconsistent contractual stipulations by virtue of Section 24, and the interest that accrues under Sections 15-17 — compound, at three times the bank rate — makes early engagement with Council proceedings the only economical course.</div>
